---
// /attribution — GENERATED from the provenance artefacts, never hand-written.
// The list here is exactly the list in each ward's provenance JSON plus the
// fixed non-footprint layers, each joined to a licence entry. A dataset with no
// licence entry fails the build (ward-record.ts), so a new source cannot ship
// un-attributed. This retires the hand-typed paragraph the source spec had,
// which had already drifted (it named sources we do not use and omitted ones
// we do).
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import StandardsPage from '../components/standards/StandardsPage.astro';
import { LICENCE_BLOCK, ODBL_NOTICE } from '../scripts/standards/odbl.ts';
import { LICENCES, allWardRecords } from '../scripts/standards/ward-record.ts';

const records = allWardRecords();
/* Keyed by LAYER + DATASET, not by dataset alone. A licence attaches to how the
   data reaches us, not to the dataset in the abstract — Google Open Buildings
   supplies both our footprints (via Overture, so ODbL governs) and our heights
   (direct from Earth Engine, so CC BY 4.0 governs). Collapsing those into one
   row per dataset forced a single licence onto two different answers, and the
   published table was wrong because of it. */
const shipped = new Map<string, {
  layer: string; dataset: string; sourceLicence: string; governingLicence: string;
  via?: string; holder: string; url: string; note?: string; wards: Set<string>;
}>();
for (const r of records) for (const l of r.provenance.layers) {
  const key = `${l.layer}::${l.dataset}`;
  const e = shipped.get(key) ?? { ...l, note: LICENCES[l.dataset]?.note, wards: new Set<string>() };
  e.wards.add(r.name); shipped.set(key, e);
}
const rows = [...shipped.values()];
const differs = rows.filter((r) => r.governingLicence !== r.sourceLicence);
const site = Astro.site!.href.replace(/\/$/, '');
const jsonLd = { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebPage', name: 'Data attribution — Delta Climate Research', url: `${site}/attribution`, isPartOf: { '@type': 'WebSite', url: `${site}/` } };
---
<Base
  title="Data attribution — Delta Climate Research"
  description="Every dataset the urban climate digital twin is built from, its licence and its holder — generated from the provenance files the map itself ships."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />
  <StandardsPage
    kicker="Attribution · Every source, its licence, its holder"
    title="What this is built from"
    lede="This page is generated at build time from the same provenance files the map serves. A dataset without a licence entry fails the build, so nothing can appear on the map that does not appear here."
  >
    <h2>What each layer is built from</h2>
    <p>Two licence columns, because they are not always the same thing. <strong>Governing</strong> is what you must honour if you reuse our data — it follows how the data reaches us. <strong>At source</strong> is what the original publisher released it under. Where they differ, the delivery path is named.</p>
    <div class="tblwrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Dataset</th><th>Governing licence</th><th>At source</th><th>Holder</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {rows.map((e) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{e.layer}</td>
            <td><a href={e.url} rel="noopener">{e.dataset}</a>{e.via && <><br /><span class="muted">via {e.via}</span></>}</td>
            <td><code>{e.governingLicence}</code></td>
            <td>{e.governingLicence === e.sourceLicence ? <span class="muted">same</span> : <code>{e.sourceLicence}</code>}</td>
            <td>{e.holder}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    {differs.length > 0 && (
      <>
        <p><strong>Where the two differ, and why it matters.</strong> Overture publishes its buildings theme as ODbL <em>as a whole</em>, so every footprint reaching us through it is governed by ODbL no matter what its contributor released it under.</p>
        <ul>
          {differs.map((e) => (
            <li><strong>{e.dataset}</strong> — <code>{e.sourceLicence}</code> at source, but <code>{e.governingLicence}</code> as we redistribute it, because the {e.layer} arrive via {e.via}.</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p>This table previously showed only the source column. For Microsoft's footprints that read as <code>CDLA-Permissive-2.0</code> — a permissive licence with <em>no</em> share-alike — which would have led a reuser to the wrong obligation entirely. The governing column is the one to act on.</p>
      </>
    )}

    <h2>Footprints, per ward</h2>
    <p>Building footprints for the Kolkata wards come via Overture Maps (ODbL), which merges OpenStreetMap, Google and Microsoft footprints and de-duplicates them by GERS id. <code>traced</code> means a human drew the outline against imagery; the rest are model output. Counts are read from each ward's provenance file.</p>
    <div class="tblwrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Ward</th><th>Buildings</th><th>By source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {records.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{r.name}<br /><span class="muted">{r.body}</span></td>
            <td><span class="n">{r.provenance.footprints.count.toLocaleString('en-GB')}</span></td>
            <td>{r.provenance.footprints.byDataset.map((d) => (
              <span>{d.dataset} <span class="n">{d.count.toLocaleString('en-GB')}</span>{d.traced && <span class="muted"> · traced</span>}<br /></span>
            ))}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table></div>

    <h2>Attribution notice</h2>
    <blockquote>
      <p>Building footprints © OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL), Google Open Buildings (CC BY 4.0) and Microsoft (CDLA-Permissive-2.0), via Overture Maps Foundation. Building heights from Google Open Buildings 2.5D Temporal (CC BY 4.0). Surface reflectance from Copernicus Sentinel-2. Canopy height © Meta / WRI (CC BY 4.0). Meteorological forcing from NASA POWER. Live ambient readings © Norwegian Meteorological Institute (NLOD / CC BY 4.0). Thermal validation from NASA ECOSTRESS via LP DAAC; height validation from NASA ICESat-2. Data are used as-is for planning and research; each source's licence travels with it.</p>
    </blockquote>

    <h2>Share-alike: we take the stricter reading</h2>
    <p>ODbL draws a line we sit close to. §4.5 exempts a <em>Produced Work</em> from share-alike; §4.4 binds a <em>Derivative Database</em> to it. A rendered map image is plainly the first. A downloadable CityJSON file or 3D Tiles tileset of building geometry looks a great deal like the second, and reasonable people read it both ways.</p>
    <p><strong>So we do not argue it.</strong> Every geometry export is declared a Derivative Database and offered under the Open Database License. If the permissive reading is correct we have given away nothing we wanted to keep — the data is open by design. If the strict reading is correct we were compliant all along. An ambiguity you can engineer around is not worth carrying.</p>
    <blockquote><p>{ODBL_NOTICE}</p></blockquote>
    <div class="tblwrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>ODbL clause</th><th>How it is discharged</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>§4.2 Notices</td><td>Every export carries the licence identifier and URI, and preserves the upstream copyright notices.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>§4.3 Notice for Produced Works</td><td>The sentence above, verbatim as the licence prescribes, on the pages and in every payload.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>§4.4 Share Alike</td><td>Exports are offered under <code>ODbL-1.0</code>. This is the clause the ambiguity turns on, and declaring it settles it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>§4.6 Access</td><td>The entire database is downloadable, free, machine-readable, at a stable URL. No alteration file is needed because the whole thing is published.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>§4.7 Technological measures</td><td>None. Static files, no authentication, no rate limiting, no DRM.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p class="muted">{LICENCE_BLOCK.disclaimer} The machine-readable version, including exactly which endpoints this covers and which it does not, is at <a href="/api/licence.json"><code>/api/licence.json</code></a>.</p>
    <p>One subtlety worth stating, because it changes the answer: Microsoft's Global ML Building Footprints are <code>CDLA-Permissive-2.0</code> taken direct from Microsoft, but the same footprints reaching us <em>inside</em> the Overture buildings theme are redistributed under ODbL. For the Kolkata wards, which come via Overture, ODbL is the licence that actually governs.</p>

    <h2>What is deliberately not here</h2>
    <p>Sources evaluated and ruled out for licence reasons, so nobody re-tests them: Open-Meteo and WAQI (non-commercial), GlobalBuildingAtlas (ODbL-derived), Google Photorealistic 3D Tiles (terms forbid analysis), Cityweft (EULA forbids product integration). For a Dubai deployment the height source is <a href={LICENCES['WSF3D']!.url} rel="noopener">WSF3D</a> (DLR, CC BY 4.0), which will appear in this table when a Dubai ward ships.</p>

    <p class="muted">Machine-readable per ward: <code>/api/wards/&#123;id&#125;/metadata.json</code> → <code>provenance</code>.</p>
  </StandardsPage>
</Base>
